WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.794
In this video you'll learn what a base

2
00:00:03.794 --> 00:00:06.824
language model actually produces,

3
00:00:06.824 --> 00:00:10.191
and why janus, who posts as @repligate,

4
00:00:10.191 --> 00:00:12.769
calls the result a multiverse.

5
00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.496
A base model gives a probability to every possible

6
00:00:16.496 --> 00:00:20.141
next token, and sampling picks one,

7
00:00:20.141 --> 00:00:23.148
again and again, so one prompt leads

8
00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.921
to a whole tree of possible texts.

9
00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:29.545
This series covers two sets of her ideas,

10
00:00:29.545 --> 00:00:31.217
four videos each.

11
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:32.633
Part one is Loom,

12
00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:35.002
her interface for exploring that tree.

13
00:00:35.002 --> 00:00:37.463
Part two is how information flows

14
00:00:37.463 --> 00:00:39.891
inside a transformer.

15
00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:43.882
A base model is a language model trained only

16
00:00:43.882 --> 00:00:47.411
to continue text, with no chat tuning on top.

17
00:00:47.411 --> 00:00:49.803
It reads and writes tokens:

18
00:00:49.803 --> 00:00:52.381
words, or pieces of words.

19
00:00:52.381 --> 00:00:54.029
Give it a prompt:

20
00:00:54.029 --> 00:00:56.119
a story about a lighthouse keeper

21
00:00:56.119 --> 00:00:59.277
who has not spoken to anyone in eleven years.

22
00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:01.448
It ends with the words "the voice

23
00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:03.201
on the other end said".

24
00:01:03.201 --> 00:01:06.893
What comes out is not a sentence but a probability

25
00:01:06.893 --> 00:01:10.191
for every token in the model's vocabulary:

26
00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:12.013
tens of thousands,

27
00:01:12.013 --> 00:01:14.544
often more than a hundred thousand.

28
00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:16.715
A few tokens get most of the weight,

29
00:01:16.715 --> 00:01:18.631
and most get almost none.

30
00:01:18.631 --> 00:01:22.288
At some points one token takes nearly everything;

31
00:01:22.288 --> 00:01:25.481
at others the weight is spread across many.

32
00:01:25.481 --> 00:01:28.825
This is the next-token distribution:

33
00:01:28.825 --> 00:01:32.193
the model's whole answer at each step.

34
00:01:32.643 --> 00:01:36.567
To get text, one token has to be picked.

35
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:38.878
That step is called sampling.

36
00:01:38.878 --> 00:01:40.642
Picture a weighted die

37
00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:43.301
with one face for every token,

38
00:01:43.301 --> 00:01:47.422
each face as large as its probability.

39
00:01:47.422 --> 00:01:50.465
Roll it. The token that comes up is added

40
00:01:50.465 --> 00:01:52.171
to the text, and the longer text

41
00:01:52.171 --> 00:01:54.667
goes back in for a new distribution.

42
00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:59.625
Roll again, append, repeat, one token at a time.

43
00:01:59.625 --> 00:02:01.912
A setting called temperature

44
00:02:01.912 --> 00:02:05.105
reshapes the die before each roll:

45
00:02:05.105 --> 00:02:08.460
low favours the likeliest tokens,

46
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.503
high gives unlikely ones more of a chance.

47
00:02:11.966 --> 00:02:13.336
Given the same text,

48
00:02:13.336 --> 00:02:16.088
the model computes the same odds every time;

49
00:02:16.088 --> 00:02:18.028
the chance is all in the roll.

50
00:02:18.491 --> 00:02:21.533
Now run the same prompt twice.

51
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:22.961
Early rolls may agree,

52
00:02:22.961 --> 00:02:25.131
but sooner or later one lands differently,

53
00:02:25.131 --> 00:02:27.268
and the two texts drift apart.

54
00:02:27.268 --> 00:02:31.275
Same model, same prompt, two different stories.

55
00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:34.511
Keep every run, and the runs form a tree.

56
00:02:34.511 --> 00:02:36.601
The prompt is the root.

57
00:02:36.601 --> 00:02:39.004
Each node is the text so far,

58
00:02:39.004 --> 00:02:42.383
and its children are the ways it could go on.

59
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:44.496
With four children per node,

60
00:02:44.496 --> 00:02:47.212
there are four texts after one split,

61
00:02:47.212 --> 00:02:50.835
sixteen after two, sixty-four after three,

62
00:02:50.835 --> 00:02:53.795
and over a million after ten.

63
00:02:53.795 --> 00:02:56.431
The number of paths grows exponentially.

64
00:02:56.431 --> 00:02:58.381
Where the text is predictable,

65
00:02:58.381 --> 00:03:01.458
the branches agree and the tree stays narrow.

66
00:03:01.458 --> 00:03:04.789
Where it is open, the tree fans out.

67
00:03:04.789 --> 00:03:07.587
That width is the model's uncertainty,

68
00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:08.936
made visible.

69
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.812
janus wrote about this in a 2021 essay,

70
00:03:12.812 --> 00:03:16.388
Language models are multiverse generators.

71
00:03:16.388 --> 00:03:19.023
In her analogy, the model plays the role

72
00:03:19.023 --> 00:03:22.622
of physics, the prompt is the present moment,

73
00:03:22.622 --> 00:03:25.687
and each sample is one timeline, as in the

74
00:03:25.687 --> 00:03:28.810
many-worlds picture of quantum mechanics.

75
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.631
In her words, a language model associates

76
00:03:31.631 --> 00:03:35.405
every prompt "not with a single continuation

77
00:03:35.405 --> 00:03:39.180
but a multiverse of continuations."

78
00:03:39.631 --> 00:03:42.174
Why do you rarely see this tree?

79
00:03:42.174 --> 00:03:45.146
First, chat apps show one reply:

80
00:03:45.146 --> 00:03:48.188
a single path, the rest never drawn.

81
00:03:48.188 --> 00:03:50.533
Second, the models behind them are tuned

82
00:03:50.533 --> 00:03:53.342
to follow instructions and chat,

83
00:03:53.342 --> 00:03:57.255
and tuned models tend to lose much of the variety.

84
00:03:57.255 --> 00:04:00.111
janus wrote about this in 2022

85
00:04:00.111 --> 00:04:02.746
under the name mode collapse.

86
00:04:02.746 --> 00:04:05.521
In one of her examples, models were asked

87
00:04:05.521 --> 00:04:09.167
for a random number between 0 and 100.

88
00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:13.765
A base model spread its probability fairly evenly.

89
00:04:13.765 --> 00:04:15.402
An instruction-tuned model

90
00:04:15.402 --> 00:04:18.861
strongly favoured one number: 97.

91
00:04:18.861 --> 00:04:21.160
Turning up the temperature, she found,

92
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.180
does not bring the variety back.

93
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.688
A base model keeps the variety,

94
00:04:25.688 --> 00:04:28.938
and it will continue any kind of document:

95
00:04:28.938 --> 00:04:33.118
a story, a manual, a forum thread.

96
00:04:33.118 --> 00:04:35.266
The next video is about Loom,

97
00:04:35.266 --> 00:04:38.077
an interface that shows you the tree.

98
00:04:38.527 --> 00:04:40.048
Keep this.

99
00:04:40.048 --> 00:04:43.252
One. At every step, a base model's answer

100
00:04:43.252 --> 00:04:47.293
is a probability for every possible next token.

101
00:04:47.293 --> 00:04:50.149
Two. Sampling picks one token

102
00:04:50.149 --> 00:04:52.215
by weighted chance and repeats,

103
00:04:52.215 --> 00:04:55.675
so the same prompt can give different texts.

104
00:04:55.675 --> 00:04:59.262
Three. Keep all the runs and they form a tree,

105
00:04:59.262 --> 00:05:02.037
what janus calls a multiverse:

106
00:05:02.037 --> 00:05:05.079
narrow where the text is predictable,

107
00:05:05.079 --> 00:05:07.298
wide where it is open.
